Alright, I need to make sure the codebase stays consistent after the recent changes. I just added the material constants (MAT) to `game.js` when I updated that file, consolidating them with the weapon and armor definitions. Previously, the HUD module (`hud.js`) was pulling `MAT` (and also `BOXES`) directly from `map.js`. Since `MAT` now lives in `game.js`, the HUD's import is outdated and will cause a runtime error—`MAT` will be undefined when the HUD tries to reference material IDs for UI elements like health bars or weapon icons. 

I should verify whether `hud.js` actually needs `BOXES`. Scanning the HUD code, I see it only references weapon data, armor pricing, and rendering utilities; there’s no usage of any map geometry or box definitions. So the `BOXES` import is unnecessary and can be dropped entirely. This simplifies the import list and eliminates a stale dependency.

The fix is straightforward: modify the import statements at the top of `hud.js`. I’ll replace the existing three-line import block:

```js
import { WEAPONS, ARMOR_PRICE } from './game.js';
import { BOXES, MAT } from './map.js';
import { crosshairCSS, renderCrosshair } from './settings.js';
```

with a corrected version that pulls `MAT` from `game.js` and removes `BOXES`:

```js
import { WEAPONS, ARMOR_PRICE, MAT } from './game.js';
import { crosshairCSS, renderCrosshair } from './settings.js';
```

I’ll use the `Edit` tool with `replace_all` set to false, targeting exactly that block. This ensures only the intended import lines are altered, leaving the rest of the file untouched. Making this change will resolve the missing `MAT` reference and clean up an unused import, keeping the HUD module aligned with the new material definition location.

Proceeding with the edit now.

